diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4385385
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
@@ -0,0 +1,124 @@
+<a id="CreditControl"><h1>Credit Control</h1></a>
+
+<h2>Overview</h2>
+<p>Credit control brings the credit decisions about customers into webERP so that the same rules are applied to every customer every time, no matter how the order arrives, and so that there is a record of what was decided, by whom and when. It covers:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li>credit reviews of customers, with the outcome, notes, a new credit limit and the next review date;</li>
+	<li>putting customers <b>on stop</b> and <b>on watch</b>, and back to normal;</li>
+	<li>holding orders for customers on stop, and orders that would take a customer over their credit limit, until credit control releases them;</li>
+	<li>reminder letters (dunning letters) in escalating stages, sent by email in a weekly run;</li>
+	<li>interest and late payment fees on overdue balances, charged at month end;</li>
+	<li>bad debt write-offs, with approval of larger write-offs;</li>
+	<li>the credit control report, the write-off report and the reconciliation of the customer accounts with the debtors control account;</li>
+	<li>a full credit control history for each customer for the auditors.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Everything credit control does to a customer is recorded in the customer's <b>Credit Control History</b>, shown at the bottom of the <b>Customer Credit Control</b> screen: reviews, status and limit changes, held and released orders, reminders, charges, waivers, disputes, exemptions, notes and write-offs, with the date, time and user. Nothing in the history can be changed or deleted.</p>
+
+<h2>Who can do what</h2>
+<p>Two security tokens control credit control:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>21 Credit control</b> - put customers on stop, on watch or back to normal, change credit limits, record reviews, release held orders, run reminders and month end charges, mark disputes, set exemptions, waive charges and request write-offs. Give this token to the role used by the credit controller and the deputy credit controller (and the Finance Director) in <b>Setup - Access Permissions Maintenance</b>.</li>
+	<li><b>22 Bad debt write-off approval</b> - approve write-offs above the approval limit. Give this token to the Finance Director's role only.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Both tokens are given to the System Administrator role when the system is upgraded. Which token is needed can be changed in <b>Page Security Settings</b> using the entries <i>CreditControlAuthority</i> and <i>WriteOffApproval</i>.</p>
+<p>Sales staff and the sales office can see credit information but cannot change it: in <b>Customer Maintenance</b> the credit limit and credit status are shown but cannot be edited without the credit control token. A user set up for a salesperson only sees the credit information of the customers of their own branches, in the credit control screens and reports as in the rest of webERP. Credit status codes themselves (<b>Setup - Credit Status</b>) can only be maintained with the credit control token.</p>
+<p>If both the credit controller and the deputy are away, any user with token 21 can act; the Finance Director should hold it for this reason.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditControlSetup"><h2>Setting up credit control</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Maintenance - Credit Control Parameters</b> (Finance, token 10):</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>Standard review period</b> - new customers get a next review date this many months after their account is opened. Shorter periods for higher risk customers are set in their reviews.</li>
+	<li><b>Credit status used for automatic stop</b> - the status given to a customer when a bad debt is written off.</li>
+	<li><b>Minimum days between reminders</b> - a customer does not get another reminder letter until this many days after the last one.</li>
+	<li><b>Interest rate</b> - simple interest, per annum, charged at month end on items more than the set number of days overdue. Zero for no interest. One rate is used for all currencies.</li>
+	<li><b>Late payment fee</b> - one flat fee per customer per month, set in the home currency and converted to the customer's currency at the exchange rate on the day the charges are run. Zero for no fee.</li>
+	<li><b>Interest income account</b>, <b>Late payment fee income account</b> and <b>Bad debt account</b> - the general ledger accounts the charges and write-offs post to.</li>
+	<li><b>Write-offs above this need approval</b> - home currency value above which a write-off waits for the Finance Director. Zero means every write-off needs approval.</li>
+</ul>
+<p><b>Setup - System Parameters - Check Credit Limits</b> must be set to <b>Hold orders for credit control approval</b> for orders over the credit limit to be held. The other settings (do not check, warn, prohibit) work as before.</p>
+<p><b>Setup - Credit Status</b> defines the credit status codes. Each code means one of:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>Normal</b> - trade normally;</li>
+	<li><b>Watch</b> - orders are accepted, but whoever takes an order sees a warning with the reason, and the order is listed for credit control;</li>
+	<li><b>On stop</b> - no new orders by any route, and nothing on existing orders is dispatched or invoiced unless credit control releases the order.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The meaning of a code that customers already have cannot be changed - create a new code and move the customers to it, so that each change is recorded against the customer.</p>
+<p><b>Receivables - Maintenance - Reminder Letter Stages</b> (Finance, token 10) sets the number of reminder stages, how many days overdue triggers each stage, and the email subject and wording of each letter. Three stages are set up to start with: a first reminder at 30 days, a second reminder at 60 days and a final notice at 90 days. The wording can use {CustomerName}, {CustomerCode}, {Currency}, {OverdueAmount}, {InvoiceList}, {Date}, {CompanyName}, {CompanyEmail} and {CompanyPhone}. Letters are in English; wording for other languages is not supported yet.</p>
+<p>Reminders go to the customer contacts flagged to receive statements (<b>Add a customer contact</b>, <i>Send statement</i>). Credit control should check every customer on account has one before the first run.</p>
+
+<a id="GoLive"><h2>Going live</h2></a>
+<p>When the system is upgraded every existing customer keeps the credit status and credit limit they have in webERP and is given a next review date twelve months ahead. Open orders of customers who are already on stop are put on credit hold. To load the credit controller's spreadsheet - the stop list, credit limits and review dates - use <b>Receivables - Maintenance - Import Credit Status and Limits</b>. The csv file has one customer per row with the columns: customer code, credit status code, credit limit, next review date (YYYY-MM-DD), reason. Leave a column empty to leave that value unchanged. Run it first with <i>Test run only</i> ticked to check the file. Every change is recorded in the customer's credit control history with the reason from the file.</p>
+
+<a id="CustomerCreditControl"><h2>Customer credit control - reviews, stop and watch</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Transactions - Customer Credit Control</b>, or <b>Credit Control</b> from the selected customer menu, shows the customer's credit status and the reason for it, credit limit, balance, how much they are over their limit, overdue amount, next review date, last reminder, and exemptions; their outstanding items; the interest and fees charged; their credit reviews; and the full credit control history. Users without the credit control token see all this but cannot change anything.</p>
+<h3>Recording a credit review</h3>
+<p>Select the outcome (limit confirmed, raised or reduced, put on watch, put on stop, returned to normal, other), enter the credit limit and credit status after the review, the next review date and notes. The new limit and status take effect immediately and are recorded with the review, so the limit and the reason for it are always together. Reviews cannot be changed or deleted - record a new review instead. The customer drops off the "due for review" list until the next review date.</p>
+<h3>Putting a customer on stop or watch</h3>
+<p>Choose the new credit status and give a reason. The reason is shown to staff taking orders for the customer. Putting a customer on stop puts all their open orders on credit hold. Taking a customer off stop does not release those orders automatically - release each one in <b>Orders on Credit Hold</b>.</p>
+<h3>Disputed items</h3>
+<p>Mark an invoice as disputed, with the reason, from the list of outstanding items. A disputed item is not included in reminder letters and is not charged interest until the dispute is marked as settled.</p>
+<h3>Exemptions and notes</h3>
+<p>Tick <i>Exempt from interest</i> and/or <i>Exempt from late payment fees</i> for key accounts or customers with an agreed payment plan, with the reason. Notes - for example a payment promise, or that a debt has been passed to a collection agency or solicitor - are added to the credit control history.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditHolds"><h2>Orders on credit hold</h2></a>
+<p>An order is put on credit hold when:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li>the customer is on stop - an order received electronically (EDI) or a change to an existing order for a customer on stop is kept but held; new orders from the sales office, customer log ons and the web shop are refused; standing (recurring) orders are not raised at all for that period;</li>
+	<li>with <i>Check Credit Limits</i> set to <i>Hold orders for credit control approval</i>, the order takes the customer over their credit limit (outstanding balance plus open orders) - whether entered in the sales office, by a customer log on, through the web shop, by EDI or raised from a standing order. A change that increases a released order is checked again.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>An order on credit hold cannot be dispatched or invoiced and is left off picking lists. The sales order list shows <i>Credit Hold</i> instead of the invoice link. <b>Receivables - Transactions - Orders on Credit Hold</b> lists the held orders; credit control releases an order with a reason. The same screen lists the orders taken recently for customers on watch.</p>
+<p>Counter sales are invoiced at once, so with orders over the limit held for approval, counter sales that would take a customer over their limit are not allowed.</p>
+
+<a id="ReminderRun"><h2>The reminder letter run</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Transactions - Reminder Letter Run</b>, normally weekly. Choose the date to work out what is overdue at (normally today), and optionally a salesperson or currency, then <b>Prepare Run</b>. The list shows each customer who will get a reminder, which stage, the overdue amount and items, and where it will be sent. Untick customers to leave out - for example where payment has been promised - and give the reason; it is recorded in their history. Then <b>Send Reminders</b>.</p>
+<p>The rules:</p>
+<ul>
+	<li>Only overdue items that are not disputed are included. Unallocated payments and credits reduce the overdue amount; a customer who has paid gets nothing.</li>
+	<li>A customer moves up one stage at a time - the first letter they get is the first stage even if they are already 90 days overdue, and the next stage is sent in a later run if they are still overdue and past the next stage's days.</li>
+	<li>A customer never gets another reminder within the minimum days between reminders.</li>
+	<li>Once everything covered by the earlier letters has been paid, the next overdue item starts again at the first stage.</li>
+	<li>After the final notice the customer is listed as <i>Final notice already sent - credit control to decide on stop</i>. Customers are not put on stop automatically by the reminder run; credit control decides.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Letters are emailed in the customer's currency, listing the overdue items. Customers without an accounts contact email address, and any email that could not be sent, are listed at the end of the run with links to print the letters for posting. Every letter is recorded against the customer with the date, stage, items and amount, can be printed again exactly as sent, and is shown in the customer's history and in the customer portal.</p>
+
+<a id="MonthEndCharges"><h2>Month end interest and late payment fees</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Transactions - Month End Interest and Late Payment Fees</b>. Choose the month end and <b>Prepare Charges</b>. The list shows, for each customer, the overdue balance, the interest and the fee in the customer's currency, the home currency total, and any notes (exempt, already charged this month). Untick any customer who should not be charged and <b>Confirm and Post Charges</b>.</p>
+<ul>
+	<li>Interest is simple interest at the annual rate on each overdue item for the days of the month it was overdue. Interest is not charged on earlier interest or fees, nor on disputed items.</li>
+	<li>The fee is one flat fee per customer per month, for customers with an item more than the set number of days overdue.</li>
+	<li>Each charge is a separate transaction - <i>Late Payment Interest</i> or <i>Late Payment Fee</i> - on the customer's account dated the month end, and appears as a separate item on the next statement. It is posted to the debtors control account and the income account set in the parameters.</li>
+	<li>A month can only be charged once to each customer for each type of charge - running the charges again for the same month charges nobody twice.</li>
+	<li>No tax is charged on interest or fees.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>To waive a charge, find it on the customer's credit control screen, give the reason and click <b>Waive</b>. A <i>Credit Charge Waiver</i> is posted to the customer's account, allocated against the charge, and reversed out of the income account.</p>
+
+<a id="WriteOffs"><h2>Bad debt write-off</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Transactions - Bad Debt Write-off</b>. Select the customer, tick the items to write off (all or part of each), give the reason and choose whether to put the customer on stop (ticked unless credit control has decided otherwise). Allocate any unallocated payments or credits before writing off what is left.</p>
+<ul>
+	<li>If the home currency value is not more than the approval limit, the write-off is posted immediately.</li>
+	<li>Otherwise it waits at the top of the same screen until someone with the write-off approval token, who is not the person who requested it, approves or rejects it. Nothing is posted until it is approved. If the items have been paid in the meantime the approval is refused.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>A posted write-off is a <i>Bad Debt Write-off</i> transaction on the customer's account at today's exchange rate, allocated against the items written off. The bad debt account is charged at today's rate and the difference from the rates the invoices were raised at is posted to the exchange difference account, so the debtors control account is reduced by exactly what the items were carried at.</p>
+<p>If a written-off debt is later paid, use <i>Written-off Debt Paid After All</i> on the same screen to record a <i>Bad Debt Recovery</i> for the amount received (this credits the bad debt account), then enter the receipt and allocate it against the recovery in the usual way.</p>
+
+<a id="CreditControlReport"><h2>Reports</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Reports - Credit Control Report</b> lists customers due or overdue for a credit review, customers over their credit limit (and by how much, largest first), customers on stop and customers on watch, with their salesperson, currency, status, limit, balance, overdue amount, next review date and last reminder. It can be filtered by salesperson and by currency, and totals are shown in the home currency. A salesperson log on only sees their own customers.</p>
+<p><b>Aged Customer Balances</b> now shows each customer's credit status, credit limit and the last reminder stage sent.</p>
+<a id="WriteOffReport"></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Reports - Bad Debt Write-off Report</b> lists the write-offs requested in a period, with the customer, amount, reason, who requested it, who approved or rejected it and when it was posted.</p>
+
+<a id="Reconciliation"><h2>Month end reconciliation with the general ledger</h2></a>
+<p><b>Receivables - Reports - Debtors Control Account Reconciliation</b> compares, period by period, the total of the customer accounts in the home currency with the debtors control account, after interest, fees, waivers, write-offs and differences on exchange. If they do not agree, the second table lists every transaction where the customer accounts and the control account differ - usually journals posted directly to the control account, or transactions posted to different periods.</p>
+
+<a id="CustomerPortal"><h2>The customer portal</h2></a>
+<p>Customers who log in see, under <b>Reminders and Charges on Account</b>, their balance, the reminder letters sent to them (which they can open) and the interest and fees charged, including any waived. A customer only ever sees their own account. Customers on stop are told that their account is on hold and to contact credit control when they try to place an order, in the portal and in the web shop. Customers are never told that they are on watch.</p>
+
+<a id="SalesStaffNote"><h2>Note for sales staff - watch and stop</h2></a>
+<ul>
+	<li><b>Watch</b> means credit control is concerned about the customer. You can still take orders. You will see a warning with the reason when you select the customer - talk to the customer about their account before taking large orders. Credit control sees every order taken for customers on watch.</li>
+	<li><b>On stop</b> means no new orders can be taken for the customer, by you, by the customer in the web shop or portal, by EDI or from standing orders, and nothing already ordered will be picked, dispatched or invoiced until credit control releases it. You will see the reason when you select the customer.</li>
+	<li><b>Over the credit limit</b> - the order is accepted but held until credit control approves it. The order list shows <i>Credit Hold</i>.</li>
+	<li>You can see the credit status, limit, balance, overdue amount and reminders of your own customers in <b>Customer Credit Control</b> and the <b>Credit Control Report</b>, but you cannot change them. Only credit control can change a credit limit or take a customer off stop.</li>
+	<li>Contact the credit controller - or the deputy credit controller when the credit controller is away - about any customer on watch or on stop.</li>
+</ul>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
